{{Quote|Collectors should keep an eye out for statues of the Four Sages of Dwartii&mdash;Sistros, Braata, Faya, and Yanjun ''[sic]''&mdash;the ancient philosophers and lawmakers who influenced the earliest laws in the Galactic Republic.|Eloc Throno, ''Traveler's Guide to Batuu''|<ref name="Traveler's Guide">''[[Star Wars: Galaxy's Edge: Traveler's Guide to Batuu]]''</ref>}}
'''Faya Rodemos''' was one of the [[Four Sages of Dwartii]], controversial [[philosopher]]-lawgivers who influenced the early laws of the [[Galactic Republic]]. A [[19 BBY|thousand years after]] the Republic's founding, a [[bronzium]] statue of Rodemos was kept by the door to [[Supreme Chancellor]] [[Darth Sidious|Sheev Palpatine's]] [[Emperor's office|private office]].

==Biography==
{{Quote|The statue of Faya Rodemos, which used to stand like a sentry by Palpatine’s office door, wears a mask that obscures the mouth, as if silencing the sage. Arms crossed in supplication or perhaps bound by an assailant, it looks as if Faya is being restrained.|A book's description of Faya Rodemos's statue|<ref name="100 Objects">''[[Star Wars 100 Objects]]''</ref>}}
Faya Rodemos<ref name="Den of Antiquities">{{GEAttr|Dok-Ondar's Den of Antiquities}}</ref> was a member of the [[Four Sages of Dwartii]], an ancient group of controversial [[philosopher]]s and lawgivers who [[Homeworld|hailed]] from the [[Inner Rim Territories|Inner Rim]] [[planet]] [[Dwartii]].<ref name="Inside Intel" /> During the [[Sith]] Order's [[Era of the Sith|reign]] over [[the galaxy]],<ref name="Den of Antiquities" /> after the [[Galactic Republic|Old Republic]] had been driven from the<ref name="Propaganda">''[[Star Wars Propaganda: A History of Persuasive Art in the Galaxy]]''</ref> [[Galaxy|galactic]]<ref name="Timelines">''[[Star Wars: Timelines]]''</ref> [[capital]] of [[Coruscant]],<ref name="Propaganda" /> statues were created of Rodemos as well as the other Sages: [[Braata Danlos]], [[Sistros Nevet]], and [[Yanjon Zelmar]].<ref name="Den of Antiquities" /> With the aid of the [[Jedi Order]], the Republic [[Jedi-Sith War|defeated]] the Sith<ref name="Propaganda" /> in [[1032 BBY]], marking a presumed end to their Order.<ref name="Timelines" />

[[File:Faya_Sheev_Anakin.png|thumb|left|275px|A statue of Faya Rodemos stood outside the private office of Supreme Chancellor Palpatine.]]
Due to the decisiveness of their victory, the Republic resettled its capital on Coruscant and re-formed itself into the Galactic Republic<ref name="Propaganda" /> that same [[Standard year|year]].<ref name="Timelines" /> Rodemos and the other Sages influenced the renewed Republic's [[politics]] in the early years of its rule,<ref name="Inside Intel" /> including its first laws.<ref name="TVE">''[[Star Wars: The Visual Encyclopedia]]''</ref> The four eventually came to be regarded as having been controversial lawmakers with contentious views.<ref name="100 Objects" /> By [[19 BBY]],<ref name="Date 2">''[[Star Wars: Timelines]]'' dates the events of [[Star Wars: Episode III Revenge of the Sith|''Star Wars'': Episode III ''Revenge of the Sith'']], which includes the appearance of Faya Rodemos' statue, to [[19 BBY]].</ref> large [[bronzium]] statues of both Rodemos and Yanjon Zelmar flanked the door to the [[Emperor's office|private office]] of the Republic [[Supreme Chancellor]] [[Darth Sidious|Sheev Palpatine]]&mdash;secretly the [[Dark Lord of the Sith|Sith Lord]] Darth Sidious&mdash;on Coruscant,<ref name="Complete Locations">[[Star Wars: Complete Locations (2016)|''Star Wars: Complete Locations'']]</ref> where it stood like a sentry.<ref name="100 Objects" /> Lining the hallway to Palpatine's quarters was a collection of slightly smaller statues of the Sages.<ref name="Inside Intel" />

In [[34 ABY]],<ref name="TGDate">{{TravelersGuidetoBatuuDate}}</ref> within his guide [[book]] for the planet [[Batuu]], ''[[Traveler's Guide to Batuu]]'', [[historian]] [[Eloc Throno]] instructed [[collector]]s to look out for<ref name="Traveler's Guide" /> a set of statuettes showcasing the Four Sages of Dwartii, Rodemos among them. The statuettes had come into the hands of the<ref name="Inside Intel" /> [[Ithorian]] [[antiques dealer]] [[Dok-Ondar]]<ref name="Traveler's Guide" /> and were put up for sale at his shop, the [[Dok-Ondar's Den of Antiquities|Den of Antiquities]],<ref name="Inside Intel" /> the same year.<ref name="Date 3">The events of [[Dok-Ondar's Den of Antiquities]] within [[Star Wars: Galaxy's Edge|''Star Wars'': Galaxy's Edge]] take place in [[34 ABY]] per ''[[Star Wars: Timelines]]''.</ref> Rodemos' statue in both collections depicted the Sage as wearing an ornate, hooded [[robe]]<ref name="Den of Antiquities" /><ref name="ROTS">{{Film|III}}</ref> and a [[Helmet|mask]].<ref name="100 Objects" /> Details about Rodemos' statue were mentioned in a book looking at the use of [[propaganda]] by various factions, ''[[A History of Persuasive Art in the Galaxy]]'', that was released shortly after the [[Hosnian Cataclysm]]<ref name="Propaganda" /> in 34 [['ABY-BBY' dating system|ABY]].<ref name="Timelines" /> A [[Unidentified historical book|book]] describing objects relating to [[Timeline of galactic history|galactic history]] included an entry on statues of the Four Sages of Dwartii, elaborating on how Rodemos' statue stood sentry-like at Palpatine's office door, as if its mask was silencing and restraining the figure.<ref name="100 Objects" />

==Behind the scenes==
{{Quote|Gert [Stevens, concept artist] did two new statues.|Production Designer [[Gavin Bocquet]], on Stevens designing the Faya Rodemos and Yanjon Zelmar statues for ''Revenge of the Sith''|<ref name="Art of Episode III">''[[The Art of Star Wars Episode III: Revenge of the Sith]]''</ref>}}
[[File:Faya_Yanjon_statue_concepts.png|thumb|250px|Concept sketches of Faya Rodemos and Yanjon Zelmar's statues by Gert Stevens]]
A statue of Faya Rodemos first appeared in the [[2005]] [[prequel trilogy]] film, [[Star Wars: Episode III Revenge of the Sith|''Star Wars'': Episode III ''Revenge of the Sith'']].<ref name="ROTS" /> [[Gert Stevens]], a concept artist for the film, drew sketches of Rodemos' as well as Yanjon Zelmar's statue.<ref name="Art of Episode III" /> In the new ''[[Star Wars]]'' [[canon]], Rodemos' first name was identified in the [[2016]] reference book [[Star Wars: Complete Locations (2016)|''Star Wars: Complete Locations'']], in a portion detailing locations from ''Revenge of the Sith''.<ref name="Complete Locations" /> That section was originally written by [[Kerrie Dougherty]] for the 2005 [[Star Wars Legends|''Star Wars'' Legends]] reference book of the [[Star Wars: Complete Locations (2005)|same name]], which also marked the first identification of "Faya" in Legends.<ref name="Complete Locations 2005">[[Star Wars: Complete Locations (2005)|''Star Wars: Complete Locations'', 2005 edition]]</ref>

It was not until [[2019]], however,<ref name="D23">{{D23|url=creatures-droids-and-aliens-meet-the-fantastical-figures-of-star-wars-galaxys-edge|text=Creatures, Droids, and Aliens: Meet the Fantastical Figures of ''Star Wars'': Galaxy's Edge}}</ref> that the surname "Rodemos" was given to Faya through the [[Star Wars: Galaxy's Edge|''Star Wars'': Galaxy's Edge]] land<ref name="Den of Antiquities" /> at [[Disneyland]] and [[Disney's Hollywood Studios]].<ref name="D23" /> At Dok-Ondar's Den of Antiquities, one of the land's retail locations, Rodemos' full name is labeled on a box containing small statues of Rodemos and Braata Danlos, which attendees can purchase.<ref name="Den of Antiquities" />

